/* ── SUS overlay / floating positioning ─────────────────────
   Companion sheet of the overlay stack (OverlayHost, SusOverlayService,
   WorldSpaceService). Attached to the FLOATING ELEMENT ITSELF by
   OverlayHost.ApplyFloatingPosition(), not to a panel-level cascade.

   Why: floating content is usually a component with its own companion
   USS (`.sus-card { position: relative }` and friends). A utility class
   living in _global.uss has the same specificity and sits in a FARTHER
   sheet, so it loses — measured in T-2653 and documented in _global.uss.
   Adding this sheet on the element makes it the nearest and last-added
   one, so `.sus-floating` wins the way the old inline
   `style.position = Absolute` did (R120/D-069).

   Only `position` lives here on purpose: left/top/width of a floating
   element are measured from the anchor's worldBound at runtime and stay
   in C# behind `sus:uss-impossible` markers.
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

.sus-floating {
    position: absolute;
}
